# SoX NG TODO — updated 2026-10-24

## Goal

Finish the fast, context-aware Bash completion for the Core 2 SoX NG package in `AmrUser-48/linux`. Global-option grammar stays hand-written. Runtime capabilities are cached per user; a normal Tab press must not start SoX.

## CI status

- GitHub Actions run [#15](https://github.com/AmrUser-48/linux/actions/runs/40127158846) passed on SoX NG 14.8.1.
- The package-installed completion test printed: `PASS: formats, effects, effect flags/values, read-only filtering, and cache reuse.`
- The package smoke tests passed an effect chain (`gain`, filters, `rate`, `dither`), the `loudness` effect, `stat -e` output checks, and native Opus encode/decode.
- That successful run also published release tag `core2-system-sox-ng-14.8.1-core2.1-20261024195252` from the feature-branch-triggered workflow. This was unintended for a feature/test run; inspect the release before deciding whether it should remain. The workflow now restricts future release publication to `master`.
- A workflow quoting bug caused Markdown backticks in release details to be executed as shell substitutions; the feature-branch workflow was fixed, and the next CI run must verify the corrected release-note behavior.


## Current implementation

- `core2-packages/sox-ng.bash-completion` — hand-written option/argument grammar; context-sensitive completion for `-t`, formats, devices, filenames, effect names, effect flags, and documented flag values.
- `core2-packages/sox-ng-completion-cache.py` — reads the installed binary’s help output and writes safe Bash arrays for available formats, read/write capability, devices, effects, effect flags, and enumerated values.
- `core2-packages/test-sox-ng-completion.sh` — completion and cache-reuse tests.
- `.github/workflows/d630-core2-sox-ng.yml` — package integration and smoke tests.
- Work is on PR #1, branch `sox-ng-bash-completion`; it has not been merged. M4A/M4B writing is out of scope; reading support already works.

## Next steps

1. **Verify the latest CI run after the release-step guard and release-note quoting fix.** It must build/package/test completion successfully and must not publish a release from `sox-ng-bash-completion`.
2. **Validate the metadata parser against the exact packaged SoX NG 14.8.1 help output.** Check `--help`, `--help-format all`, and `--help-effect all`; inspect whether every format’s read/write status and every device/effect name is parsed correctly.
3. **Fix and verify completion grammar.** Check which global options actually take arguments; distinguish global options from per-file format options; ensure `-t` suggests read formats for inputs and writable formats for outputs; ensure `-m/-M/-T` are not treated as argument-taking options; test paths with spaces and partially typed `--option=value`.
4. **Test real effect syntax and parameters.** Include `gain`, filters, `dither`, `stat`, the installed loudness/EBU R128 functionality, and any optional effects compiled into this package. Do not hard-code an effect or flag as supported if the installed build does not report it.
5. **Verify cache invalidation and latency.** The first completion may query SoX to build the cache; later Tab presses should use the cache only. Check invalidation after executable, completion, and dynamic-handler updates, plus concurrent cache creation.
6. **Review package integration.** Confirm the completion files and cache generator land at the intended paths, Bash loads both `sox` and `sox_ng` completion names, and Python is only needed for cache refresh—not each Tab press.
7. **Run the completion test script against the packaged binary, fix any failures, and re-check the PR diff.** Only then mark the PR ready; do not merge without the successful CI result.

## Acceptance criteria

- No SoX subprocess on ordinary Tab presses after cache creation.
- Global option grammar is maintained by hand; dynamic formats/devices/effects/effect options come from the installed SoX NG binary.
- Read-only formats are never suggested as output formats.
- Effect flags and documented enumerated values are completed in effect context without being confused with global/file-format options.
- Tests pass using the package built by the workflow, not only a different host SoX version.
